.TH UNEXPAND 1 "2026" "spicule" "spicule User Commands"
.SH NAME
unexpand \- convert spaces to tabs
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B unexpand
.RB [ \-a ]
.RB [ \-t
.IR tablist ]
.RI [ file ]\ .\|.\|.
.SH DESCRIPTION
.B unexpand
copies each
.I file
(or standard input, if none is given) to standard output, converting
<blank> (<space> or <tab>) characters at the beginning of each line
into the maximum number of <tab> characters followed by the minimum
number of <space> characters needed to reach the same column
positions. The leading run of <blank> characters on each line is
always converted this way, regardless of its length or of whether
.B \-a
was given.
.PP
Each <backspace> character is copied to the output unchanged, and
decrements the column position used for tab stop calculations; the
column position is never decremented below the first column.
.SH OPTIONS
.TP
.B \-a
In addition to the leading run on each line, also convert every other
run of two or more <blank> characters that immediately precedes a tab
stop, anywhere in the line. A single <blank> character is left
unconverted even if it lands exactly on a tab stop.
.TP
.BI \-t " tablist"
Set the tab stops used for the conversion, instead of the default of
every 8th column, using the same
.I tablist
grammar as
.BR expand (1)
(a single interval, or a strictly ascending comma/blank-separated
list of column numbers).
.I tablist
may be given as a separate argument or attached directly to the
option
(\fB\-t\fR\fItablist\fR).
Giving
.B \-t
enables the same additional mid-line conversion that
.B \-a
enables; the two options may be combined, with no additional effect
beyond either one alone.
.SH OPERANDS
.TP
.I file
A pathname of an input file. If
.B \-
is given, standard input is used at that point in the operand
sequence.
.SH EXIT STATUS
.TP
.B 0
All input files were copied successfully.
.TP
.B 1
An input file could not be opened; the remaining operands are still
processed.
.TP
.B 2
An option or its argument was invalid.
.SH SEE ALSO
.BR expand (1),
.BR tabs (1),
.BR cat (1)
